==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|līden}}, from {{inh|de|goh|līdan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*līþan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*līþaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|lijden}}, {{cog|en|lithe}}. The sense developed from “go, travel” via “endure” to “suffer”. This happened under the influence of the originally unrelated noun {{m|de|Leid|t=sorrow}}, which is cognate with {{cog|en|loath}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈlaɪ̯dən/|[ˈlaɪ̯dn̩]}}
* {{audio|de|De-leiden.ogg|audio}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|leiden<litt,gelitten>}}

# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[bear]]; to [[endure]]; to [[undergo]] (some hardship)
#: {{ux|de|Lerne '''leiden''' ohne zu klagen.|Learn '''to suffer''' without complaining.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[suffer]]; to feel [[pain]]
# to [[suffer]] (from; a disease or condition) {{+preo|de|an}}
#* '''2012''' April 20, ''{{w|Die Welt}}'' [https://web.archive.org/web/20130412065211/http://epaper.apps.welt.de/welt/archiv], page 22:
#*: {{quote|de|Durch Passivrauchen steigt bei Kindern das Risiko, dass sie als Erwachsene an einer chronisch-obstruktiven Lungenerkrankung '''leiden'''.|By passive smoking, the risk increases in children that they suffer from chronic obstructive lung disease as adults.}}

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj|leiden<litt,gelitten>}}

